Z||000002||Black88GT||09-08-2001||03:48 PM||||Against it.  They don't use it enough to be effective.  They use it more on poor people then rich people (better lawyers = less penalty).  <P>Lethally injecting someone  to die a peaceful death is supposed to be a punishment of some sort?  Seems to me its the easy way out rather then sitting in a 8x8 cell for 23 hours a day.   <P>Just my 2 cents.||||reg||1
Z||000003||annieL||09-08-2001||03:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by hugh:<BR><B>You know where I stand! Against it!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah I know, I should of said everone but Hugh!! JUST KIDDING HUGHIE<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||fogglethorpe||09-08-2001||03:49 PM||||There are alot of variables here. For instance, what crime or crimes would make a person worthy of extermination? Should it be under state or federal jurisdiction? What about the Constitutional issue? Article VIII in The Bill of Rights protects Americans from "cruel and unusual punishment". Who decides what is cruel and unusual? Maybe death itself could be considered that by some, or maybe some would see the electric chair as cruel, but not lethal injection.<BR>My heart tells me that some of the heinous acts, such as murder, deserve death; but, as a Libertarian, I have a problem with Government sponsored and initiated killing.<BR>This is a very difficult moral issue...I hope this answer is understandable.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||hugh||09-08-2001||04:03 PM||||Black,Exactly how I feel.As for the rest of you guys,What is this a family reunion?||||reg||1
Z||000006||My7Fieros||09-08-2001||04:22 PM||||This is a pretty heavy subject for a Fiero forum..even for the off topic section. It def has the potential to start a heated debate also. But since you asked, Im for it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mach10||09-08-2001||04:51 PM||||As long as everyone keeps it clean, no reason it can't be discussed.<P>Pros: <BR>-Cheaper than storing people in jail for life<BR>-Good deterant<BR>-Final justice for some pretty awful crimes<P>Cons:<BR>-How are we better than the criminal by ending his life?<BR>-What if he/she WASN'T guilty?<P>In all, I am still sitting on the fence. In some cases (Tim McVeighs) I feel that it was MORE than warranted. Others, I'm not so sure.<P>Personally, I'd rather corporeal punishment rather than capital punishment. That, and make inmates work for their upkeep, like in some European countries. Our highways could use some resurfacing. Cut the prison terms in half, lay on a few dozen lashes at the start of the sentencing, and there you have it: A penal system that is beneficial to those OUTSIDE the law, and extremely uncomfortable for those inside.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||hugh||09-08-2001||05:08 PM||||Mach,McVeigh is no longer feeling anything,it's as if he never existed.If he had been put in prison for life,he would suffer everyday for the next 40 or 50 years.THAT'S PUNISHMENT.Also,I've read that it cost's more to enforce the death penalty because of the appeals process.||||reg||1
Z||000009||annieL||09-08-2001||05:12 PM||||I want to say i'm for it. I have had someone I cared for murdered. He only got 7 years. I feel he should of gotten a lot longer than that. They only found him guilty of manslaughter so of course he got a lesser sentence.<BR>I am not for giving someone an appeal after appeal (unless there is proof he/she may not be guilty). The person they murdered never got a chance to appeal. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||maryjane||09-08-2001||05:19 PM||||So, ya want a Texan's opinion on the death penalty, huh? Was kinda hoping this question would never surface here. Actually, I don't think it is a really big deterrence to crime. 10 or 15 yrs ago I would have said "Fry 'em". I've mellowed in the face of the #'s of inmates exonerated by dna testing, and by the # of the very young facing death from crimes committed when they were 16-17 yrs old or younger. I guess I would be for it, in extreme cases, after ALL<BR>available investigations are done. And I mean fair and impartial investigations. I've always thought a prosecuter should try his/her best to be sure they have the right person, not just rush to get a conviction. I reserve the option to change my vote after I think about this awhile. Count mine-undecided.  ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mach10||09-08-2001||05:24 PM||||Hugh: I respect your dislike of the death penalty. all the same, it just doesn't seem right to let that remorseless animal continue his worthless existence on this planet. Unfortunately, I also know that his passing cannot atone for the hundreds of his victims. I think "ravaged by wild dogs" would have been a fitting end.<P>annieL: *applause*<P>I'd like to take the stance of "Pro-Justice" Stop treating criminals like decent citizens. By breaking the law, you should be forfeighting just about every right until you have served your sentence. In addition, the punishment should fit the crime. 7 years for murder is a slap on the wrist. <p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 09-08-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000012||maryjane||09-08-2001||05:38 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>Against it. <P>Lethally injecting someone  to die a peaceful death is supposed to be a punishment of some sort?  Seems to me its the easy way out rather then sitting in a 8x8 cell for 23 hours a day.   <P>Just my 2 cents.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sitting in a cell is not always a punishment<BR>especially for life-long criminals. Remember Charlie Manson's reply, when asked by the prosecutor how he was going to like life imprisonment? "If you give sombody the whip, and they like the whip, what have you really done to hurt em?, You're just sendin me home." Something like that anyway. Of course this doesn't apply to someone who is actually remorseful of their deed/s.<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 09-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||starfighter007@msn.com||09-08-2001||06:04 PM||||Sittin on a fence on this one .Only to be put to death if the criminal admits guilt to the crime.If not no way .Too many innocent  people have been put to death.||||reg||1
