Z||000015||rogergarrison||09-08-2001||08:09 PM||||get rid of them, there convicted and a drain on the rest of us. I think Ive heard it cost us $50k a year to take care of a deathrow inmate. I say give him his 500,000 volts and give me a tax refund.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>In fact, the one thing I agree with arabs on ......do what they did to their victim. Shooting them, drowning them, hanging them, whatever. They wack off hands of thieves for example. Maybe that will make em think twice.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 09-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||DaRkLoRD||09-08-2001||08:45 PM||||we had this discussion in one of my college classes last year (political science... an elective)<P>In the case of a violent, unprovoked crime, I favor the death penalty.. then again, except in the cases of serial killers, I can't think of anything that's not provoked in some way.. but I have no doubt that others can think of many examples.. our society is too full of stress, people get angry about things and often have no way to deal with it.<P>personally, I would prefer death over being put into a cage.. but that's because I believe in reincarnation.. so death would be sort of like a reset button, with a built in memory erase (whoa, only a geek would say that. hehe)<P>anyway, I have a lot more I'd like to say, but I don't really feel like typing it all. since no one ever emails me when I say to email if you want to discuss this further, I won't bother saying it.. I will say this however.. if I was in a position of political power... the remaining jails would be a lot less crowded.... (and there would be a large demand for psychotherapists...)<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000017||beer_stud_21||09-08-2001||09:06 PM||||This subject was covered in a college class of mine at one point too.  Personally I am aginst.  If only to make certain that there is no way an innocent man could be put to death.  I cannot thing of a worse thing than to have your life snuffed out by the government for a crime you did not commit.  <P>Some other random thoughts:<P>The death penalty does not deter (sp?) crime.  At the moment someone is about to commit a crime that would be punishable by death they are either A) Not in the frame of mind to think about the concequences of their actions.  Or B) They dont feel they will be caught, or dont care if they die or not.  <P>If non violent, non repeat offenders were not thrown in prision for years and years for crimes that never physically harmed anyone we would spend no more money no our prisions as we do right now.  Probably less.  For example:  An honest hardworking, tax paying citizen gets thrown in jail for growing a dozen or so marijauana plants.. What good is there in taking a man like this away from his family and his job and locking him up for 30-40 years?  Its totally beyond me.<P>But this is just a 20yr olds opinion...<BR>-Marlin...  icq # 8273989<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||theogre||09-08-2001||09:08 PM||||I don't have much of a problem with a death penalty per say.... The problem is making 100% sure you are frying the right individual. I have little doubt that the wrong person has been executed on at least a few occasions.<P>Deterent? not likely. look at the kind of crime involed in the majority of cases. A big issue is that once they are gone they can't preach to followers. You can't write to a corpse like you can to someone Manson. Now at least most victems sue so these twisted F___s can't write books and make money from it.<P>"People" like Timmothy McVey (spelling?) aren't salvagable. The inmate that whacked Jeffrey Dahlmer did us all a favor. There was no question at all those clowns did what they did.<P>Free Mumia? now there's a much harder one. That's one where I'd have to see all the evidence and a few more things. Philly, like many other cities was much diferant then. There is a legitimate concern that they grabbed the first chump they thought they could tie it to. That case has been very racial from the start and will stay so until the end. (Mumia Abu Jahmal for those who don't know is a case that the Delaware Valley region has been dealing with for around 20 years.)<P>There have been far too many instances of proven police coruption in the U.S.. L.A. is still cleaning up such a mess.<P>Pilly had another corruption mess just a couple years ago, and more recently they busted a bunch robbing corpses in one of the Philly area coroners offices. What's to make one believe they didn't also screw up evidence?<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||fierobaby||09-08-2001||09:11 PM||||I have mixed feelings on the death penelty... all depends on the crime...you know... like Timathy Mcveigh (SP?) he was definatly on my DIE list... but some I guess no they should die...l can't really think of one now... so I guess that means I am for the death penelty?<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="LETTER"> AIM: Fierobaby87<BR>http://angelfire.com/sports/sfiero </FONT><BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cfiero.JPG"> ||||reg||1
