Z||000034||beer_stud_21||09-09-2001||03:01 AM||||<B>I apologize. I DO not think that it is an issue. I know that most people on death row are guilty, there may be a "small" (and I mean very small) fraction of innocent people.</B><P>Yeah, ok.  But how would you feel to be that "small" percentage.  To be taken away from your family and friends and essentually murdered for some hienious (sp?) crime that you did not commit!!  Personally I dont think I can think of anything worse.<P><B>He can't be too honest if he's growing pot plants in his backyard. However 30-40 years is a long time for drug possesion. Some murderers get less time than that.</B><P>The point is the guy isn't hurting anyone.  There are millions of honest americans that "use" pot in this country.  A good portion of them have never and will never hurt another soul in their lives.  But the government says its against the law and will go to great lengths to arrest someone that likes to sit around at home and get high.........  ;-(<P>-Marlin<p>[This message has been edited by beer_stud_21 (edited 09-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000035||FieroKyle||09-09-2001||04:01 AM||||For it, if you kill a person to just kill a person, you shall pay with your own life. The only thing I hate about it is that our justice system isn't perfect so sometimes innocent people get executed. Just because of all this new DNA testing, many have been found innocent and in Indiana (or Illinois, I forget) they stopped all executions because they found many people that were on death row innocent. But I am still for it.||||reg||1
Z||000036||operator64||09-09-2001||04:19 AM|||| sorry for the long post.   This is a prime example of poor punishment for the crime.                                                                                                                                                                                    Dustin Camp has gone from freedom to jail and now to prison.<P>Potter County Sheriff's deputies firmly and quickly escorted an apparently stunned Camp out of the courtroom Friday after 108th District Judge Abe Lopez ruled he had violated five aspects of his probation for killing Brian Deneke by hitting him with his Cadillac.<P>Lopez ordered Camp, 20, to prison for eight years.<P>"I think we are just a little sick of the finger being pointed at everyone but Dustin Camp," said Assistant 47th District Attorney John Coyle. "The time has come to accept responsibility, and the time has come for this court to enforce the judgment of the jury."<P>According to testimony in the murder trial from a passenger in Camp's car, Camp said "I'm a ninja in my Caddy" and "I bet he liked that one," after driving over Deneke during a 1997 fight in the Western Plaza shopping center.<P>Camp testified at that trial he feared for the life of a friend at the hands of Deneke during the melee where "punks" with studded collars and extravagant hair were fighting with "preps" who appeared more conservative.<P>A Potter County jury originally sentenced him to 10 years in prison and a $10,000 fine for the manslaughter conviction, but suspended the sentence pending Camp's successful completion of his 10-year probation.<P>Some of Camp's probation violations included consuming alcohol, being in a place where crimes were happening, breaking curfew and fleeing from police.<P>Defense attorney Warren Clark said Camp's 62 days in the Potter County Detention Center was punishment enough for these violations.<P>"What man is not guilty of these kinds of transgressions in his youth?" Clark said. "His actions are consistent with reckless, rash youth."<P>Clark made an impassioned plea to the judge.<P>"I'm asking - I'm unabashedly begging - you to find there is still hope for Dustin Camp," he said. "This is not a throw-away kid. There are many who have stirred the pot in this case, and I'm asking you to be strong."<P>Coyle said that Camp's behavior "demonstrated a lack of respect for the jury that gave him an incredible second chance."<P>"The defendant himself testified here less than two years ago he could abide by all the conditions of probation if only the jury would give him a second chance," Coyle said.<P>Camp's probation violations occurred on the night of June 26 and the early morning of June 27. Canyon police responded to a call reporting disorderly conduct at a home at 811 Oregon Trail.<P>Camp was in the home drinking beer where officers suspected there were minors drinking, said officer Manuel Torres, during testimony Thursday.<P>Debra Gaines, Camp's probation officer until last week, testified Camp admitted to drinking at the gathering and to fleeing out the back door of his brother's home when police arrived.<P>Attorney Jeff Blackburn represented the Deneke family in a civil suit in which the Camp's insurance company paid $20,000 to the Denekes. Blackburn sat with the family during Thursday's hearing.<P>"I can't rejoice in anybody going to the penitentiary, but I can't be sad for this kid," Blackburn said. "This is the closest to justice this case has seen."<P>The original trial attracted national attention, with network television reports on ABC's 20/20 and NBC's Dateline.<P>Mike Deneke, the dead man's father, said the new sentence and upcoming documentaries on the case would send a message to the world "not to judge people by the way they appear."<P>Deneke also said the family was still waiting for "some real indication of acceptance of responsibility," by Camp.<P>Clark could not be reached for comment on whether he will appeal his client's sentence.<P>The Camp family could not be reached for comment.<P>Rebecca King, 47th District attorney, doesn't anticipate an appeal.<P>"It was a very clean trial.... I would be a little surprised," she said.<BR>||||reg||1
