Z||000040||Urchin||09-09-2001||05:51 PM||||Since this thread was introduced I have considered whether I am for or against it.  I am still to this point undecided.  I think the reasons for and the reasons against are all fairly obvious.  There is one point I did want to address though.  In reference to "killing an innocent man/woman"...  First of all, that is uncommon.  But looking at this issue a little closer... well this may be a rather distasteful way to illustrate it but, as the expression goes...  "you can't make an omlette without breaking a few eggs".  When you balance the number of innocent lives that may be taken at the hands of lethal injection or whatever the method used, against the numbers of possible victims upon release, parole or escape of the criminal in question (assuming we are discussing murderers).  I think that the number of innocent people put to death would be substancially lower.  This does not mean that I am for it.  I can see both sides.  But I thought that this may be worth mentioning.  Though, I doubt many will agree with me on this. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/side_cut_sig_4.jpg">  <BR>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||1
Z||000041||beer_stud_21||09-09-2001||08:31 PM||||Urchin,<P>I see your point, but I still think there is no excuse for executing even 1 single innocent person.  If it were me I would eliminate capitol punishment for that reason alone.  As for the fact that the murderers get out of jail and kill more innocent people.  That point is moot.  Anyone who has commited a crime punisable by the death penalty would never be let out on parole should the death penalty be done away with.  Also..  For every person wrongly conviced of a crime and executed there will be the real perpitrator still at large(unless they are already in prision on other charges of course).  Obviously if someone was executed for a crime the case would be closed and whoever really did it could very well still be out there commiting more crimes.  <P>Just something more to think about.<P>-marlin||||reg||1
Z||000042||maryjane||09-09-2001||09:02 PM||||I'm still undecided on this and probably will remain so. It would be real easy to say I'm for it, but my conscious won't allow it. But to declare against it, I would feel obligated to offer a viable alternative. I believe I read recently that the U.S. prison population is approaching 8% of total population. The present system obviously isn't working. In leiu of the death sentence,I might be able to accept a life sentance w/no possible chance of parole,at extremely hard labor. No ammenities<BR>whatsoever should be allowed, including communication with other inmates, or anyone from the outside. It would amt to federalized slavery of sorts.  Even then a person could entertain themselves in their mind; more than their murdered victims could do. If it were extreme enough, the survivors of the victims might go alng with it, but would the ACLU? Would it clear the Constitutional hurdles?  Lot's of questions, few real answers.<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000043||RoadRocket||09-09-2001||09:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>I don't have much of a problem with a death penalty per say.... The problem is making 100% sure you are frying the right individual. I have little doubt that the wrong person has been executed on at least a few occasions.<P>Deterent? not likely. look at the kind of crime involed in the majority of cases. A big issue is that once they are gone they can't preach to followers. You can't write to a corpse like you can to someone Manson. Now at least most victems sue so these twisted F___s can't write books and make money from it.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Ogre, I'm afraid I have to both agree and disagree with you.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>First, you are absolutely correct, in cases where there is no doubt, the death sentence should apply. In cases where there is doubt, life without parole is best.<P>Second, deterent is not a good word for it, but that is what it is. I, however, prefer to think of it as "consequences". <BR>In other words, if you commit this crime, this is what it will cost you if you are caught and convicted.<P>Does this stop everyone from murder? Of course not. There are some who, for what ever reason, will continue to do what they are gonna do. Some people will always be beyond reach.<P>This penalty is to stop those who _can_ be stopped, as it is with any other penalty for a crime.<BR>Without a system of deterents, civiliation could not sustain itself. It would collapse into anarchy as people did whatever they pleased, with little or no consequences to themselves.<P>Just my two cents...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Ed Dana<BR>88 Coupe.||||reg||1
Z||000044||ray b||09-09-2001||09:40 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by annieL:<BR>[B]   Considering that the defense of the accused also has a say in picking a potential juror your claim to a state rigging the jury is totally off base.<BR>And if i'm not mistaken the state will only ask a juror if they are for or against the death penalty if they are trying a death penalty case. You can't pick a new boss if everybody from the "family" is in jail.<P>reply<BR>so in the death penalty case only those that favor the death can be on the jury and you see that as fair and i see it as stacked againts <BR>only randon jurys are fair, if anyone is kicked off it is stacked by them doing the kicking, the state prostituting att.[yes i know how to spell prosecuting]<BR>in the first mob post it was about letting killers off scotfree for telling tales so i guess some of the mob [the rats] will still be free and will be the new boss.<BR>itis unreal to think they will get all of the mob anyway<BR>kinda funny sad that pro-lifers are mostly pro-death penality tooo.<P><P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000045||beer_stud_21||09-09-2001||10:05 PM||||<B>kinda funny sad that pro-lifers are mostly pro-death penality tooo.</B><P>I've noticed that too.  But I don't think we should get into an abortion discussion.  That would make for to big/stressful of an arguement.  A lot of the pro lifers are adament to the point of getting violent about their position of that issue.  I would hate to piss one off.  I wouldn't want to have my name posted of one of those hate lists online and have to watch out for snipers for the rest of my life....<P>-marlin||||reg||1
Z||000046||annieL||09-09-2001||10:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>[QUOTE]Originally posted by annieL:<BR>[B]   Considering that the defense of the accused also has a say in picking a potential juror your claim to a state rigging the jury is totally off base.<BR>And if i'm not mistaken the state will only ask a juror if they are for or against the death penalty if they are trying a death penalty case. You can't pick a new boss if everybody from the "family" is in jail.<P>reply<BR>so in the death penalty case only those that favor the death can be on the jury and you see that as fair and i see it as stacked againts <BR>only randon jurys are fair, if anyone is kicked off it is stacked by them doing the kicking, the state prostituting att.[yes i know how to spell prosecuting]<BR>in the first mob post it was about letting killers off scotfree for telling tales so i guess some of the mob [the rats] will still be free and will be the new boss.<BR>itis unreal to think they will get all of the mob anyway<BR>kinda funny sad that pro-lifers are mostly pro-death penality tooo.<P>ray b: I do not make up the rules that apply to picking a juror. If you have a problem with how that is handled contact your state government. I did not say a state will reject a juror if they do not believe in the death penalty. You are forgetting that both sides of a case get to pick who sits in the jury box. If the defense attorney for the accused dosen't like a potential juror than he can reject him or her. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000047||frontal lobe||09-09-2001||10:23 PM||||To even put the pro-life issue in the pro-death penalty thought process is BEYOND absurd.  Why are you even trying to compare the fate of completely innocent people (or if you don't want to accept them as people, completely innocent cells) to the fate of murderers?  I don't get it.  You can't make that distinction??<P>kinda funny sad that people that are against the death penalty would not be vegetarians!!!!!!  (intentionally absurd statement to illustrate the point)<P>Please, I am not PERSONALLY attacking rayb or beer_stud_21 or anyone else.||||reg||1
Z||000048||Fierowrecker||09-09-2001||11:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by frontal lobe:<BR><B>To even put the pro-life issue in the pro-death penalty thought process is BEYOND absurd.  Why are you even trying to compare the fate of completely innocent people (or if you don't want to accept them as people, completely innocent cells) to the fate of murderers?  I don't get it.  You can't make that distinction??<P>kinda funny sad that people that are against the death penalty would not be vegetarians!!!!!!  (intentionally absurd statement to illustrate the point)<P>Please, I am not PERSONALLY attacking rayb or beer_stud_21 or anyone else.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>HMMM...<BR>I am pro-life, and anti-death penalty...<BR>But I do eat meat...<BR>I even raised the hogs in my freezer...<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
